.TH SAPHIRA-PROFILES 5 "September 2026" "Saphira Linux" "File Formats"
.SH NAME
saphira-profiles \- install profile format for saphira-bootstrap
.SH DESCRIPTION
An install profile declares
.B top-level package intentions
for one kind of Saphira system. Profiles are consumed by
.BR saphira-bootstrap (8),
which installs them with the package manager into an empty target
directory. Image creation
.RB ( saphira-image )
and disk installation
.RB ( saphira-install )
assemble their root filesystems from the same profiles through the
same code path, so a profile that builds a working image always
installs a working system.
.SS Format
One entry per line. Blank lines and lines starting with
.B #
are ignored. Any other line is either a package name or a profile
include:
.TP
.I package
A package name to install: letters, digits, dot, underscore, plus
and hyphen only.
.TP
.IB @ name
Include the profile called
.IR name :
its entries are expanded in place, recursively, with reference-cycle
detection. The
.IB @saphira-stage4- name
spelling includes the profile called
.IR name
(the historical
.IB @akadata-stage4- name
spelling is still accepted).
.PP
Duplicate packages across includes are installed once.
.SS The profile invariant
A profile lists
.B only
explicit intentions. It must never enumerate resolved dependencies:
dependency closure belongs to APK metadata
.RB ( depends ),
not to the image definition. A profile that duplicates the package
database rots the moment any dependency changes; a profile of
intentions stays correct because the resolver re-proves it on every
install. Validate a profile by resolving it against the repository
index and proving every transitive dependency is satisfiable \-
before touching image assembly.
.SH PROFILES
One tree,
.IR /usr/share/saphira/profiles/ ,
is the single authority. Image creation
.RB ( saphira-image )
and disk installation
.RB ( saphira-install )
consume it through the same code path; there is no second profile
namespace.
.TP
.B base-system
Init-neutral Saphira system: the package and bootloader material every
installed machine carries, and no init system at all. Compose it with
.B init-openrc
or
.B init-systemd .
.TP
.B init-openrc
.TQ
.B init-systemd
The init system for a Saphira machine, and nothing else. Naming the init
separately is what keeps
.B base-system
reusable: a base that named an init would force every caller to restate it.
.TP
.B minimal-openrc
.TQ
.B minimal-systemd
The minimum for a genuinely bootable, manageable machine: base, the
kernel line, the init, the bootloader, filesystem tooling, remote
access and timezone data. The two differ only in the init package.
.TP
.B saphira-openrc
The Saphira 0.2.1 base system with OpenRC: the minimal set grown into
a product (product identity, network framework, DHCP client,
firewalling, ACPI handling, editor, ping).
.TP
.B saphirad-systemd
The SaphiraD 0.2.1 base system with systemd: the systemd twin of the
above (product identity differs, so the init choice is visible inside
the running system).
.TP
.B role-networking
.TQ
.B role-dns
.TQ
.B role-firewall
.TQ
.B role-remote-access
Role layers: additional intention lists composed on top of a minimal
profile. Roles are profile data, not meta-packages: no new package
name is invented for a role.
.TP
.B role-zfs
ZFS kernel module and userspace for a flavor whose root lives on a
pool. Required for every ZFS root (the engine refuses one whose
target lacks it); the module line follows the kernels the product
flavors pin.
.TP
.B saphira-installer
The installer userspace: enough software to INSTALL a machine, and
deliberately not enough to be one. It carries no kernel and no init
system. The bootable installer payload is this profile plus a kernel plus
an initramfs.
.TP
.B image-creator-appliance
The USB image-creator appliance: a bootable environment whose sole
purpose is creating working Saphira images. OpenRC, BIOS boot, menu
on the console instead of firstboot, sshd with root login, and the
ZFS module plus userspace so it can create pools. Its products are
chosen at runtime, never here.
.PP
Note the naming distinction, because the two are one keystroke apart:
.B base-system
is a profile, and
.B saphira-base
is an APK. A profile is only ever referenced with a leading
.BR @ ,
so
.B @base-system
and
.B saphira-base
never collide in parsing, in documentation or in an installer's output.
.PP
Note also that
.B openssh
is a single package carrying both the client and
.BR sshd .
There is no
.B openssh-client
or
.BR openssh-server ,
and a profile naming either is naming something that does not exist.
.TP
.B base
Minimal bootable skeleton: ABI marker, layout, shell and core tools.
.TP
.B network
Base plus network diagnostics and firewalling.
.TP
.B server
Network plus the hosted-service stack.
.TP
.B build
Server plus compilers and packaging tools.
.TP
.B virtualisation-host
First image-architecture profile: a boring x86-64 systemd
virtualisation host.
.RS
.PP
.nf
saphira-base
saphira-kernel
systemd
saphira-virtualisation
saphira-network-host
openssh
.fi
.PP
.BR saphira-base ,
.BR saphira-virtualisation
and
.B saphira-network-host
are metapackages: they carry no payload and exist only to give a
name, owned by APK metadata, to each dependency stack. The kernel
and the init system are named here on purpose \-
.B saphira-base
stays kernel-agnostic and init-neutral so later profiles (for
example an OpenRC image) can reuse it unchanged.
.RE
.SH FILES
.TP
.I /usr/share/saphira/profiles/
Profile directory read by
.BR saphira-bootstrap (8)
(overridable with
.BR SAPHIRA_PROFILE_ROOT ).
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR saphira-bootstrap (8)
